name sat-02-picture-split-1
timeout 520
time 1991-01-19T08:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# MEASURING RIG, NOT A CAMPAIGN RUNG. `sat-*` files belong to no `.fbc`, are named by no campaign and
# move no doctrine. They exist because 33 of the 34 campaign cells are NOT GRADUABLE: their outcome
# class (V, M) — fb_fitness.side_key, summed over the declared side — does not move when the doctrine
# genome moves, so no doctrine shift can be demonstrated on them. Format: doc/missions/syntax.md.
#
# WHAT HOLDS THIS CELL AT THE TIPPING POINT, and it is ONE quantity: THE OBJECTIVE IS THE SORT'S OWN
# OUTPUT. Each sweep member declares `kill unit <TWO NAMED MiG-29>`, the eight names cover the whole
# hostile picture, and the pairs are scrambled across both groups — pb1 takes ia1 and ib3, pb2 ia4 and
# ib1, pb3 ia2 and ib4, pb4 ia3 and ib2. The flight therefore cannot score by killing eight MiG-29s in
# any order; each of its eight bits is met only if the RIGHT member reached the RIGHT contact, and which
# member reaches which is decided by `pilot/FBFlightPicture::Assign` — one target per member out of a
# shared picture — and by nothing else. The campaign rungs all declare `kill team hostile`: ONE bit for a
# whole flight, met by no lever and unmet by all of them. Eight named units are eight independent bits,
# and the (V, M) sum resolves every one of them.
#
# THE RATIO IS THE THRESHOLD, [SET] at 2.0: eight contacts against four shooters, in two groups of four,
# both inside one 40 nm radar gate. Below 1.0 the sort is trivial — every member gets a target and the
# assignment carries no information. At 2.0 half the picture must be REFUSED, and a member that takes the
# wrong half fails a bit its wingman could have met. The scramble is deliberate so that no briefed
# contract is the right answer by construction: neither `left` nor `near` maps onto the pairing.
#
# THE SCRAMBLE IS ALSO WHAT KEEPS THE RUN ALIVE. An actor's loss ends the run unless some other actor
# DECLARED an objective covering it (doc/missions/runtime.md, `ExpectedLoss`). Naming only four of the
# eight would let a kill on either of the other four cut the run, and every number below would then be a
# function of WHEN it was cut instead of what the flight did.
#
# THE RELEASE HALF is a CCIP pair, and it is here because `pilot_attack_bias_s` and `pilot_attack_ccip_m`
# have no ground half to act on otherwise. Mk 82 and not Mk 84 because [MESS, mk82-radius.fbm] the 87 kg
# warhead's fail radius against `target_soft` is 45 m against the Mk 84's 100 m, and [MESS, sat-01
# draft 1] both bias alleles measure INERT against the Mk 84. CCIP and not CCRP because
# `pilot_attack_ccip_m` is read ONLY in `FBDeliveryMode::Ccip` (pilot/FBPilot.cpp). [MESS, this geometry]
# the pass lands 24.7 and 36.1 m long with 21.6 m of cross error, and a bias step of 0.1 s is 23 m of
# track at 231 m/s — so `ptgta` is placed 50 m BEYOND its striker's steerpoint, which makes its baseline
# impact fall SHORT and `bias-early` the allele that loses it, while `ptgtb` sits on the steerpoint and
# `bias-late` is the allele that loses that one. Two aim points, two alleles, two distinct classes.
#
# GENE FAMILIES, LIVE AND INERT — stated in advance, then measured (the arena's own table, 25 levers):
#   live    G1 shape 6 of 6:  shape-abreast (13,7)  shape-trail (13,7)  shape-tight (13,8)
#                             shape-wide (12,7)  shape-flat (10,6)  shape-stacked (14,9).
#                             The whole family grips because the station IS the picture: a wingman holds
#                             it inside `Intercept` while he has nothing assigned (doc/formation.md 4.3),
#                             so where he stands decides what his own radar can offer the sort.
#   live    G6 bias  3 of 3:  bias-early (13,7)  bias-late (11,6)  bias-rail (12,7)
#   live    G7 ccip  1 of 2:  ccip-tight (10,4);  ccip-open INERT (2 000 m accepts what 60 m already did)
#   live    G3 net   1 of 2:  net-off (14,10);  `sort-left`/`sort-near` are dead text beside a live net
#   live    G5 emcon 1 of 3:  emcon-wide (14,10);  emcon-tight/mid INERT
#   INERT   G2 cover 0 of 3:  the AIM-120 binds 0.3 s — `flt_defer_s` = 0 in 2 464 runs on 154 cells
#                             (doc/doctrine-evolution.md). Inert by AIRFRAME, not by this file.
#   INERT   G4 energy 0 of 3: it lives only inside `Phase::Bfm`, and no unit here flies `set task bfm`
#   BASELINE (14, 8).  12 movers of 24, 11 of them excluding the broken `bias-rail` stop; 9 distinct
#   classes; modal share 52.0 % (13 of 25). S1 and S2 both hold.
#   S7: [MESS] 0 of 8 spawn perturbations on the 0.8 m grid flip the baseline class — the only cell in
#   this tree that survives the chaos screen clean (w3-09-saturation flips 1 of 8). The reason is
#   structural and is the point of the file: [MESS] the baseline run reaches its full 520 s timeout with
#   no blue aircraft lost, so no verdict here is a function of the truncation instant.
#
# THE ARENA: six F-16 — a four-ship sweep and a CCIP pair — against eight MiG-29 under a GCI brief and
# two soft aim points. `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4). Every coordinate is [SET].
# `wx fixture` is the committed GFS blob and is a real atmosphere. Day, 11:00 local, sun about +33 deg
# [DERIVED]: the merge is EYED, unlike every W3 merge, and deliberately so — a night merge removes
# sensors/FBVisualSystem from the picture entirely and with it one of the inputs the sort competes for.
# THE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION: the MiG-29 is the strongest Soviet-pattern fighter in the tree, so
# RED is stronger here than any historical eight-ship this geometry resembles. No claim is made that it
# resembles one — this file is an instrument, not a rung.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: WITH TWO CONTACTS PER SHOOTER AND EIGHT NAMED TARGETS SCRAMBLED ACROSS BOTH
# GROUPS, HOW MANY OF THE EIGHT DOES THE SORT ACTUALLY DELIVER?
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   telemetry_pb*.csv   `flt_src`, `flt_assign`, `flt_switch`, `flt_dup` — the sort under a 2:1 picture.
#                       `flt_dup` > 0 is the duplicate the cost function flags and does not resolve
#   telemetry_pa*.csv   the CCIP half: `releases`, `aimErrM`
#   events.log          `flight SORT_ASSIGN` per member and tick, `sms LAUNCH_SOLUTION` per shooter,
#                       `stores DELIVERY` (aimLongM is the axis the bias gene moves), and
#                       `mission OBJECTIVE unit=pb* kind="kill unit ..."` — the eight bits that matter
#
# READING RULE, BINDING: exit 1 = FAIL and exit 3 = TIMEOUT are BOTH expected and NEITHER is the
# measurement. THE MEASUREMENT IS (V, M) SUMMED OVER THE SIX F-16 — eight `kill unit <named MiG>`, two
# `kill unit <aim point>` and six `survive`, 16 objectives — AND ITS SPREAD ACROSS THE LEVER SET. A
# reader who reports a total kill count has answered a question this file does not ask: killing four
# MiG-29s while meeting none of the eight named bits scores ZERO here, and that asymmetry IS the
# instrument. A single run measures nothing; it is one point of a distribution.

unit pb1
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight sw 1
  spawn 33.20000 43.90000 6000 90.0 480
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 80
  set fcr_mode crm
  set fcr_range_nm 40
  set rwr on
  set datalink on
  set store 1 aim120
  set store 2 aim120
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 4 aim120
  set store 6 aim9
  set store 7 aim9
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 33.20000 45.10000 6000 480
  objective kill unit pmia1
  objective kill unit pmib3
  objective survive

